/* Pass 13 C: deepen Maui's community, memory, relationship and flood arcs. */
(function(){"use strict";const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const revisions={"the-shore-i-choose":{"maui-29":{"number":29,"title":"A Festival Without a Hero","content":"The children wanted to build a stage.\n\nMaui wanted to build them a magnificent stage.\n\nTamsin wanted to know who would be responsible when the magnificent stage collapsed.\n\nWe were back where we'd started.\n\n\"The Tidewake festival needs something special,\" Maui insisted, spreading his arms toward the shoreline.\n\n\"It needs a place where people can stand without falling through the boards,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"That can be special.\"\n\n\"Then start there.\"\n\nThe Tidewake festival was a small celebration marking the first safe passage of the spring tide. It had never been a grand event. Families made food, boat crews checked the moorings, and the children decorated the upper landing with paper fish.\n\nThis year, after the repairs and the Closed Bells inquiry, the children wanted a place to tell their own stories.\n\nNot a throne for Maui.\n\nA stage for everyone.\n\nLio, Fen's apprentice boatbuilder, had volunteered to help. They brought a sketch showing a low platform with a ramp, removable railings, and a storage space underneath for the paper decorations. It would be small enough to move when storms came.\n\nMaui examined the plan.\n\n\"I could build a bigger one.\"\n\nLio looked at him.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nHe opened his mouth.\n\nClosed it.\n\n\"Good question,\" he said.\n\nI bit back a smile.\n\nWe held a planning meeting beside the garden. The children wanted to perform little scenes about their favorite parts of the cove. One wanted to tell the story of the day the fish escaped the market. Another wanted to act out the time Fen fell into the water while testing a rope. Fen was not enthusiastic about that one.\n\nPip suggested the stage include a wide entrance so children carrying props didn't have to climb steps. Edda asked for a clear route around the audience seating. Tamsin set load limits. Danse offered to check the fasteners.\n\nMaui offered to lift the platform into place.\n\nThen he looked at Lio.\n\n\"Would you like help with that?\"\n\nLio grinned.\n\n\"Yes. But only when we've measured it.\"\n\nProgress.\n\nWe spent the next week building.\n\nThe children painted the panels, argued about which fish should be blue, and changed the program seventeen times. Maui helped carry the heavy boards but left the design decisions to the people who would use the stage. I helped write the schedule, then caught myself trying to make every child equally enthusiastic.\n\nSome wanted to perform.\n\nSome wanted to paint.\n\nSome wanted to watch.\n\nThat was enough.\n\nOn the morning of the festival, a storm rolled over the ridge.\n\nRain came in thin, sharp lines. The upper path grew slick. Fen checked the tide and declared that the lower landing would remain closed until the wind eased.\n\nMaui looked at the sky.\n\n\"I could blow the clouds away.\"\n\nAang, who had come to help with lanterns, raised an eyebrow.\n\n\"Not without moving the weather into somebody else's garden.\"\n\nMaui sighed.\n\n\"You're all ruining my dramatic instincts.\"\n\n\"That's the plan,\" I said.\n\nWe moved the festival into the sheltered hall by the square.\n\nThe stage had been built in sections, so the volunteers could carry it along the level route without risking the beach steps. Tamsin inspected each joint after assembly. The children rearranged their decorations around the room, delighted by the way the paper fish looked against the old stone.\n\nThe first performance began late.\n\nA little girl named Mira stood at the center of the stage and forgot her lines.\n\nThe audience waited.\n\nMaui, seated beside me, leaned forward.\n\nI touched his sleeve.\n\nHe stayed still.\n\nMira looked toward Pip, who held the cue cards.\n\nPip smiled and lifted the first line where she could see it.\n\nMira began again.\n\nThe room cheered when she finished.\n\nMaui looked at me.\n\n\"That was harder than fighting monsters.\"\n\n\"Letting her do it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\nThe afternoon was full of mistakes. A paper fish fell off its string. Two children swapped roles in the middle of a scene and improvised an entirely new ending. Fen's fall into the water became a comedy involving a bucket and a very offended crab.\n\nFen laughed despite themself.\n\nMira found me beside the stage while the adults were packing the costumes. She held a torn paper fish against her chest.\n\n\"Did he really want to tell one of his stories?\" she asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Did I take his place?\"\n\nI crouched so she didn't have to look up at me.\n\n\"No. He decided he wanted to listen to yours.\"\n\nShe studied the fish, then glanced at Maui, who was struggling to fold a canvas screen without getting tangled in its ropes.\n\n\"He's not very good at that,\" she observed.\n\n\"He's learning.\"\n\nMira went over and asked if he needed help. He looked ready to say no, but after a moment he let her show him where the corner tied. She was proud of that in a way the applause hadn't quite managed.\n\nTamsin approached with the inspection board. The stage had passed its test, but one of the supports needed removing before the next tide. The children wanted to keep the platform for another day.\n\n\"We can ask them what they'd like to save,\" I suggested. \"The drawings can come down. The boards can be stored.\"\n\nTamsin nodded and asked the children. They chose to keep their paper fish and leave the stage pieces to the repair crew.\n\nMaui walked up carrying the folded screen.\n\n\"They didn't ask for a statue,\" he said, looking genuinely astonished.\n\n\"They asked for a place to tell a story.\"\n\n\"Which they got.\"\n\n\"And they'll need it again someday, perhaps. We can ask next time.\"\n\nHis hand found mine. He looked as though he wanted to say something grand, then leaned close enough to whisper.\n\n\"Do you think I did well?\"\n\n\"I think you listened when it was hard.\"\n\n\"That's almost praise.\"\n\n\"It is praise.\"\n\nHe kissed my forehead after checking my face for an answer. I smiled. The room around us was full of people taking their work home, children with their own stories and adults who would still need to inspect the stage in the morning. None of it existed solely so Maui could learn a lesson, and that made the celebration feel real.\n\nMaui never took the stage.\n\nAt supper, someone asked whether he would tell one of his famous stories.\n\nHe looked toward the children.\n\n\"They've got plenty of their own.\"\n\nThe person seemed surprised.\n\nThen they nodded.\n\nWe ate together while the rain softened outside.\n\nLater, when the hall was nearly empty, Maui found me by the little stage. He was carrying the spare hooks and a roll of tape, putting away the last of the decorations.\n\n\"Well?\" he asked.\n\n\"Well what?\"\n\n\"Did I behave?\"\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"High praise.\"\n\n\"You didn't rescue the play when Mira forgot her lines.\"\n\n\"Nearly did.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe looked at the floor.\n\n\"I wanted to be useful.\"\n\n\"You were. You helped build the platform. You carried it safely when the weather changed. You let the children own their own day.\"\n\nHe considered that.\n\n\"Does that count as a heroic deed?\"\n\n\"Only if you don't make a speech about it.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"Can I tell you a secret?\"\n\n\"Absolutely.\"\n\n\"I liked watching you sit in the audience.\"\n\nHe looked surprised.\n\n\"Really?\"\n\n\"Yes. You looked happy when somebody else got the applause.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"I was.\"\n\nWe stood beside the dark stage, the last lantern burning over the doorway.\n\n\"Do you want to go home?\" he asked.\n\n\"In a minute.\"\n\nHe leaned down and kissed me, warm and unhurried. I held the front of his coat and felt him smile against my mouth.\n\nWhen we pulled apart, he whispered, \"What are we waiting for?\"\n\n\"Nothing.\"\n\n\"Then why the minute?\"\n\n\"Because I like being here with you.\"\n\nHe went quiet.\n\nThen he rested his forehead against mine.\n\nWe stayed until the lantern keeper came to close the hall, at which point Maui offered to carry the hooks back to storage and got assigned a mop instead.\n\nHe used it without complaint.\n\nThe next morning, Mira left a small paper fish on our doorstep.\n\nOn the back she'd written: THANK YOU FOR LETTING ME TELL IT.\n\nMaui put it beside the wooden fish on the shelf.\n\nHe looked at both of them for a long time.\n\nI didn't ask what he was thinking.\n\nI think I knew.","word_count":1402},"maui-30":{"number":30,"title":"The Edge of the Storm","content":"The shore gate passed the first inspection and failed the second.\n\nDanse stared at the measurement as if the number had insulted him personally.\n\nToph stood beside the retaining wall with one hand resting against the stone.\n\n\"Told you,\" she said.\n\n\"You told me it was moving,\" Danse answered. \"You didn't tell me how much.\"\n\n\"I can't see your little ruler from here.\"\n\n\"You don't need to see it. You need to give me a repeatable observation.\"\n\nI looked at the tide chart.\n\nA storm was moving toward Tideglass. The old service passage beneath the cove remained sealed, but pressure readings at the lower gate had changed. The engineers had agreed to inspect the upper shore gate before the next heavy tide. It was the safest route for moving supplies away from the water, and the hinge had been repaired after the last flood.\n\nThe first measurement showed it opening freely.\n\nThe second, taken after the stone support had been dampened, showed the gate dragging against its frame.\n\nThe difference was small.\n\nIt was enough.\n\n\"Can we use it tonight?\" I asked.\n\nDanse looked at the figures.\n\n\"Not under full load.\"\n\nToph placed her palm against the support.\n\n\"The stone's shifting when the hinge takes weight. I can feel the pressure move through the wall.\"\n\nDanse shook his head.\n\n\"The displacement is within the margin for an unloaded gate.\"\n\n\"That's not the same as safe,\" Toph said.\n\nI felt the old temptation to pick whichever answer sounded more certain. Danse had calculations. Toph had experience that didn't fit neatly on the page. Both were trying to protect people.\n\nFen arrived with the updated tide prediction.\n\n\"The strongest surge will reach the lower landing after dark,\" they said. \"We need a route for the families by the cove.\"\n\n\"Then we close the gate and use the ridge path,\" I said.\n\nTamsin looked up from the hinge.\n\n\"The ridge is longer.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Some people will need assistance.\"\n\n\"Then we ask what they need and arrange it.\"\n\nDanse looked at me.\n\n\"You're making a decision before the test is complete.\"\n\n\"I'm deciding not to use an uncertain route during a storm. We can finish the test without putting people through it.\"\n\nHe considered that.\n\nThen he nodded.\n\n\"That's defensible.\"\n\nToph grinned.\n\n\"Look at that. The numbers and the dirt both agree.\"\n\nI almost laughed.\n\nThe work didn't end there.\n\nWe had to tell people the gate was closed, not merely hang a sign at a height half the village couldn't see. Pip wrote the notice. Edda checked the upper route for turning space and places to rest. Greg offered to carry lanterns but not to work past his agreed shift. Mara packed food for anyone who might need to wait at the hall.\n\nMaui watched the preparations, his hook resting against his shoulder.\n\n\"I can carry people over the wall,\" he said.\n\n\"If somebody asks and it's safe,\" I answered.\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"Right.\"\n\nThe storm arrived early.\n\nRain moved across the cove in sheets. The sea turned the color of iron. I helped Fen check the last marker by the signal post, then returned along the upper path as the wind grew strong enough to push at my coat.\n\nAt the hall, people were arriving in small groups. Some had chosen to leave their homes temporarily. Others stayed where the buildings were safe. We did not force anyone through a Threadway, and nobody had to explain why they preferred a particular place to wait.\n\nThe lanterns flickered.\n\nA child began to cry.\n\nMaui crouched beside her and asked whether she wanted a story. She nodded. He told her about a crab so vain it demanded a crown from the moon.\n\nShe laughed through her tears.\n\nThe lights went out halfway through the ending.\n\nFor a second the room was entirely dark.\n\nThen someone lit a hand lantern near the door. Another light appeared by the kitchen. Danse sent a message from Brasswatch: the generator had failed under the surge, but backup lamps were available and repairs were underway.\n\nWe counted people without making a public list of private destinations.\n\nEveryone in the hall was accounted for.\n\nThe hall went quiet after the bell. Fen kept their hand on the tide chart, waiting for the signal to end. The note faded slowly, like a cup humming after someone had touched the rim.\n\nA child asked whether the sound meant the lower gate was opening.\n\n\"No,\" Fen said. \"We don't have evidence of that. The old passage remains sealed. The pressure may be changing at a different point.\"\n\nThe answer was less exciting than a story about a door beneath the ocean. It also let everyone stay where they were instead of running toward a tunnel nobody had authorized them to enter.\n\nA volunteer brought in a fresh lantern and checked the path to the shelter entrance. The first sign had slipped in the wind. Edda pointed out that the replacement blocked the handrail when someone held it upright.\n\n\"Shift it left,\" she said. \"There. Now it gives directions without becoming another obstacle.\"\n\nWe moved two crates from the hallway. The pantry workers had already set aside food for the overnight watch, and the blankets Maui was carrying came from a labeled shelf that would need replenishing when the storm ended.\n\nI wanted to go outside and see what the waves were doing. I also knew that my wanting to witness danger didn't make me useful to the people around me.\n\n\"Could I help with the head count?\" I asked Neri.\n\n\"Yes. But use the private check-in sheet. We don't post people's destinations.\"\n\nMaui watched the process, blankets held carefully against his chest.\n\n\"All this work before anything happens,\" he said.\n\n\"That's how we give people choices if something does,\" Neri answered.\n\nHe looked at me over the last bundle. \"I suppose tearing the gate apart would be quicker.\"\n\n\"Quickly worse, maybe.\"\n\nHe laughed, then turned to help a visitor find the warm room. When he came back, he took my hand for one moment, not to claim my attention but to ask whether I was all right.\n\n\"I am,\" I said. \"I'm frightened, but I'm here.\"\n\n\"So am I.\"\n\nFor once that was the whole heroic speech. The next bell did not sound. We stayed until the watches were assigned and the lanterns were checked again.\n\nOutside, the second bell sounded.\n\nIt came from beneath the sea, deeper than the public bell, and held its note long enough to make the floor tremble. Fen looked toward the windows.\n\n\"Pressure change,\" they said.\n\n\"Can we do anything?\" I asked.\n\n\"Not safely from here.\"\n\nMaui stood.\n\nI saw the fight move across his face. His instinct was to run toward the sound and tear apart whatever held it.\n\nThen he looked at the children, the lanterns, and the people who had chosen to wait.\n\n\"What do you need me to do?\" he asked.\n\nFen handed him a stack of dry blankets.\n\n\"Take these to the west bench. Then stay where we can find you.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nThe storm passed after midnight. The lower landing was flooded, but the closed shore gate held. Its frame had shifted another fraction under the load, confirming the earlier test. Had we used it heavily, the hinge might have jammed.\n\nNo one had to discover that while standing in the doorway.\n\nAt dawn, Danse and Toph returned with Tamsin to examine the damage.\n\nDanse crouched beside the gate.\n\n\"The wet-load tolerance was inadequate,\" he said.\n\nToph tapped the stone.\n\n\"That's a polite way of saying it moved.\"\n\nHe looked at her.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"Good. Now let's fix it.\"\n\nI leaned against the railing, exhausted.\n\nMaui came up beside me with two cups of Iroh's house tea.\n\n\"How's the hero business?\" I asked.\n\n\"Terrible. I carried blankets.\"\n\n\"Very glamorous.\"\n\n\"I also told a crab story.\"\n\n\"That sounds like the important part.\"\n\nHe handed me a cup.\n\nWe stood in the cool morning air. The sea was still rough, and the ridge path needed clearing. There would be repairs, funding meetings, and another round of inspection before the gate reopened.\n\nMaui brushed his fingers against mine.\n\n\"Do you wish you'd let me break the lower passage open?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Neither do I.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\nHe was watching the water, not the crowd.\n\n\"That surprises you?\" he asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\nHe smiled tiredly.\n\n\"I've been paying attention.\"\n\nI leaned into his shoulder.\n\nThe storm hadn't ended the mystery. It had made the evidence more urgent and the repairs more expensive.\n\nWe would have to decide what to do next with the people who lived here.\n\nFor now, the gate was closed because we had chosen safety over certainty.\n\nThat felt like the right kind of courage.","word_count":1465},"maui-31":{"number":31,"title":"Six Different Roads","content":"By the time I returned to Tideglass, I had tea leaves in one pocket, a spare bolt in another, and three appointments written on the back of a bakery receipt.\n\nMaui examined the collection when I emptied it onto the kitchen table.\n\n\"Did you rob the village?\"\n\n\"Only the parts that were freely offered.\"\n\nHe held up the bolt.\n\n\"Romantic.\"\n\n\"Danse thought it might help Tamsin with the shore gate.\"\n\n\"The tea?\"\n\n\"Iroh gave me the last of the house blend for Fen.\"\n\n\"And the receipt?\"\n\n\"That's my calendar, apparently.\"\n\nHe sat down.\n\n\"You're going to need a larger pocket.\"\n\nI laughed, then stopped. I was exhausted in a way sleep alone wouldn't fix. I'd spent the last few days moving between districts, helping with the storm repairs and trying to keep promises to people I loved.\n\nI wanted to tell Maui everything.\n\nI didn't know where to begin.\n\nHe slid a cup toward me.\n\n\"Sit.\"\n\nI sat.\n\n\"Tell me the part I should know,\" he said.\n\nNot the part he had a right to own. Not every conversation, kiss, disagreement, or private fear from the other relationships.\n\nThe part I wanted to share.\n\nI wrapped my hands around the cup.\n\n\"Severus and Neri are still reviewing the Closed Bells records. They found a scraped signature, but they won't guess whose it was.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Iroh's terrace drains were making strange sounds before the storm. He thinks the blocked service route may be pushing water into places it never used to go.\"\n\n\"Less good.\"\n\n\"Danse and Toph disagree about the shore gate, but they're finally using the same measurements.\"\n\nMaui smiled.\n\n\"That sounds like a miracle.\"\n\n\"Don't call it that. They'll both be offended.\"\n\nHe laughed.\n\nI told him Katara had returned from the South and was helping the clinic organize temporary supplies across the closed bridge. Aang was making sure the emergency travel rules didn't become an excuse to control people. Greg had offered to bring chairs and lanterns to the hall, but only after checking with the volunteers.\n\n\"And you?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"What about me?\"\n\n\"What did you need?\"\n\nI looked at the table.\n\nThat was the question I'd been avoiding.\n\nI had spent so much energy helping other people have boundaries that I had begun treating my own as a nuisance.\n\n\"I need a day,\" I admitted. \"A day where nobody asks me to hold a key, fix a schedule, or make a decision.\"\n\nMaui nodded.\n\n\"Then let's have one.\"\n\n\"I don't want to cancel plans.\"\n\n\"Do you need to?\"\n\nI unfolded the bakery receipt. Two commitments could be moved without anyone suffering; the third was a clinic delivery that somebody else had already offered to cover.\n\nI wrote three short notes.\n\nThen I looked up.\n\n\"Are you sure you want to spend a day with me doing nothing useful?\"\n\nMaui grinned.\n\n\"I am a demigod. I can survive boredom.\"\n\n\"That remains untested.\"\n\nWe sent the notes before I could change my mind.\n\nThe next morning we slept late.\n\nThere were no bells. No urgent knocks. The sea was quiet enough that I could hear the wind moving through the little gaps around the window frame. Maui made breakfast with too much cinnamon and blamed the spoon.\n\nI ate it anyway.\n\nWe walked along the upper ridge, where the storm had left seaweed tangled around the stones. The lower gate remained closed for inspection. The new garden beds were damp but intact, and Fen's little patch of wild flowers had survived.\n\nMaui stopped beside the bench.\n\n\"Can I ask something difficult?\" he said.\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Do you ever wish there were only one road?\"\n\nI looked out over the cove.\n\n\"Sometimes I wish the roads weren't so complicated.\"\n\n\"That's not what I asked.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nHe waited.\n\nI spread the receipt on the table. The back was covered in small, unromantic obligations: a confirmed visit with Iroh, a message to Severus about the archive, a possible afternoon at Brasswatch, and a reminder to ask Katara whether the clinic still needed the clean blankets.\n\nMaui stared. \"It looks like an invasion plan.\"\n\n\"It's a calendar.\"\n\n\"Those are nearly the same.\"\n\nI laughed and smoothed the curling paper. He leaned close without trying to read the parts I kept folded.\n\n\"Which of these actually affects us?\" he asked.\n\n\"The visit I promised you on Saturday. The dinner I said I'd cook tonight. And the fact that I won't be here tomorrow morning.\"\n\nHe nodded, trying to take in the difference between knowing the important plans and knowing everything. We'd talked openly about my ethical non-monogamy and preference for polyamory; the trick was living that agreement when feelings weren't tidy.\n\n\"Can I say I want more of your Saturdays?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes. And I can tell you which ones I want to spend here.\"\n\n\"What if I don't like the answer?\"\n\n\"Then we can be disappointed without punishing each other.\"\n\nHe considered that for a while. \"Do you ever feel guilty because there are six roads?\"\n\n\"Sometimes. Usually when I imagine somebody wants me to solve their sadness by giving away mine.\"\n\nMaui's expression softened. \"That sounds exhausting.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe fetched the bowl of sea-glass stones we'd collected along the shore. He picked one, worn smooth and shaped a little like a heart, and set it beside the receipt.\n\n\"I don't want to be a road you have to walk because you promised.\"\n\n\"Then don't make yourself one.\"\n\n\"Could I be a place you choose to rest?\"\n\nThe question caught me off guard. I reached for his hand.\n\n\"You already are,\" I said.\n\nHe smiled, quieter than usual. For a moment the kitchen was only a kitchen, full of crumbs and papers and two people learning not to count affection like a debt. Then he asked whose turn it was to wash the dishes, and the moment improved considerably.\n\nI thought about the people I loved. Iroh's patient warmth. Severus's precise questions. Katara's steadiness, Aang's hope, and Toph's refusal to let anyone flatter her into silence. Danse learning to be a protector without a rank. Greg finding music in the quiet. Maui standing beside me, trying not to turn his own fear into a demand.\n\nNone of them was interchangeable.\n\n\"Do you wish I loved only you?\" I asked.\n\nHe looked down.\n\n\"Sometimes I wish loving you were easier.\"\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\n\"But that's different from wanting you to be somebody else.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe stared at our fingers.\n\n\"I don't need to know everything you do with everyone else,\" he said. \"I want to know what affects us. When plans change. When you're afraid. When you want me close or need me to step back.\"\n\n\"That sounds fair.\"\n\n\"And I want you to tell me when I'm asking for too much.\"\n\n\"I will.\"\n\nHe smiled faintly.\n\n\"Even if I complain?\"\n\n\"Especially then.\"\n\nWe sat on the bench for a long time.\n\nThe sea looked deceptively smooth from the ridge. Out beyond the harbor, the current still moved toward the old overflow channel. The second bell had not sounded since the storm.\n\nI wasn't going to pretend that meant it was safe.\n\nMaui leaned against me.\n\n\"Tell me something that has nothing to do with the village,\" he said.\n\nI thought about it.\n\n\"Greg wrote a song that starts with the sound of a ferry bell.\"\n\n\"That's still the village.\"\n\n\"Severus puts his books in order by a system that makes sense only to him.\"\n\n\"That's probably a crime.\"\n\n\"Toph called my kite a potato.\"\n\nHe laughed so suddenly I nearly spilled my tea.\n\n\"Was it?\"\n\n\"A graceful potato.\"\n\n\"Now I need to see it.\"\n\nI pulled the little sketch from my notebook. He studied it with great seriousness.\n\n\"Terrible,\" he said.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Absolutely terrible.\"\n\nI kissed him.\n\nHe caught my hand and kissed me again, slow and warm. The breeze lifted my hair, and the sun moved across the cove. For once I didn't feel the need to explain how this moment fit into the rest of my life.\n\nIt was part of it.\n\nOn the way home we stopped at the market. Mara had fresh bread. Fen was arguing with a supplier about the cost of rope. Lio showed us the corrected seat on the skiff and asked Maui to check a knot.\n\nHe did, then handed the line back.\n\nNo grand rescue.\n\nAt home, I took the bakery receipt from my pocket and copied the appointments into the proper calendar. I left a blank day for myself next week, without filling it immediately.\n\nMaui noticed.\n\n\"Another day of doing nothing?\"\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThat night I wrote a note in the green notebook I'd found in my old room.\n\nI am not divided into six people because I love more than one.\n\nI am one person with different roads to walk, and I get to decide when to rest.\n\nMaui read the note only after I invited him.\n\nHe didn't add a speech.\n\nHe drew a tiny fish underneath, carrying a very large cup of tea.\n\nI laughed and closed the book.","word_count":1518},"maui-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Shape of an Agreement","content":"The calendar had survived salt spray, one dropped kettle, and three months of Maui writing appointments in letters large enough to be seen from the harbor. It did not survive his attempt to improve it.\n\nI found it on the kitchen table with a line drawn through Thursday.\n\nThursday had been ours.\n\nHe had replaced it with a note: *Helping Fen. Necessary.*\n\nI stood there holding a parcel from Moonroot Archive, still wearing the coat I'd put on that morning to come home early. We'd planned the evening two weeks ago. Not a festival, not a rescue, not a shift at the pantry. Dinner, the little blue blanket, and absolutely no public meetings.\n\n\"Maui?\"\n\nHe came in carrying a coil of rope. \"Before you say anything, the new mooring—\"\n\n\"You canceled our evening.\"\n\nHe looked at the calendar. \"Moved it. Friday's open.\"\n\n\"Friday Iroh and I are reviewing the terrace map.\"\n\n\"Then Saturday.\"\n\n\"Saturday you promised Lio you'd inspect the skiff.\"\n\nHis smile faltered. \"Right.\"\n\nI set the parcel beside the stove. The air smelled like wet rope and rosemary. I wanted to say something spectacularly unfair, something about being last on his list. A familiar, uglier part of me wanted to win the argument before he knew it had begun.\n\nInstead I said, \"You didn't ask me.\"\n\nMaui put down the rope. \"Fen needed someone to help secure the landing before the wind shifts.\"\n\n\"Fen could need help. I might even agree that it's urgent. Neither changes the fact that you decided what happened to my evening without telling me.\"\n\n\"I thought you'd understand.\"\n\n\"I do understand the mooring. I don't understand why you didn't send a note.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. \"I was busy.\"\n\n\"So busy you had time to rewrite the calendar.\"\n\nThat landed.\n\nHe looked at the enormous letters he'd written across Thursday. \"Okay. That was bad.\"\n\nI pulled out a chair. \"Sit with me?\"\n\nHe sat, awkwardly. His injured hand had healed, but he still curled his fingers when he was ashamed, as if bracing for a blow that wasn't coming.\n\n\"I made a mistake,\" he said. \"I should have asked.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"I need to know something, too.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"Sometimes I look at that calendar and feel like I'm asking for an appointment with someone who has six lives. Then I hate myself for thinking it, because you told me from the beginning that you're ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I agreed to that knowing what it meant.\"\n\n\"I don't have six lives,\" I said. \"I have one. It's the same me coming to your door.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Knowing doesn't mean you stop feeling lonely.\"\n\nHe looked at me, surprised.\n\nI turned the calendar around. Its edges were curled. Under my appointments he'd added tiny drawings of fish and hooks. Beneath my private time with Severus, he'd written *No questions*. He had been trying, in his own untidy way, to respect agreements he didn't always find easy.\n\n\"Can we write down what we actually mean?\" I asked.\n\n\"Without making it a contract that gets me arrested?\"\n\n\"Preferably.\"\n\nWe took a clean sheet from the green notebook. Across the top I wrote *Our present agreements—not ownership, not forever.*\n\nMaui leaned over to read it.\n\n\"That's a long title.\"\n\n\"We can edit.\"\n\nWe agreed to tell each other about schedule changes before altering shared plans, unless there was an immediate emergency. In that case we would send word as soon as safely possible. We agreed not to share details about my other partners' private lives without their permission. He could ask for reassurance, and I could answer honestly without ranking anybody.\n\nMost importantly, either of us could say no to a date, a conversation, a kiss, or a request for time. No explanation was required to make a no count.\n\nMaui studied the last line.\n\n\"If you say no because you need quiet, can I ask when we might talk again?\"\n\n\"Yes. That's a request, not a demand. Sometimes I won't know.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"And if I need quiet?\"\n\n\"Then you get it too.\"\n\nWe tried the new agreement on a small problem before declaring it useful.\n\n\"Suppose Fen needs you during our next dinner,\" I said.\n\nMaui looked at the list. \"I ask whether it's an emergency.\"\n\n\"And if it is?\"\n\n\"I tell you where I'm going if there's time. I send word when I can. I don't decide what you'll do with the evening.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"And if it isn't?\"\n\n\"You can still choose to help, but you ask whether we can move our date before you strike it through.\"\n\nHe made a face. \"That means you might say no.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Terrible system.\"\n\nI poked him with the pencil. \"Try again.\"\n\nHe laughed. \"That means I need to decide what promise I'm keeping instead of assuming you'll wait.\"\n\nThe distinction mattered more than I expected. He could care about Fen, and he could care about me. Nobody had to become the villain because two good things asked for the same hour.\n\nI had told him I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory, but a relationship agreement wasn't just about who we kissed. It was about whether he could need reassurance without demanding my freedom, and whether I could choose to be with him without pretending all my other commitments had vanished.\n\n\"Would you like me to come with you to check the mooring tomorrow?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes. If you want to.\"\n\n\"I can do the morning. In the afternoon I'm at Moonroot.\"\n\nHe wrote both parts down. His handwriting was so large that the last word disappeared into the margin.\n\n\"There's no room,\" he complained.\n\n\"Then we'll need another page.\"\n\nHe looked at the green notebook and reached for a blank sheet. \"Are you sure we can change this later?\"\n\n\"We have to be able to. Otherwise it becomes another door we're afraid to open.\"\n\nHe nodded, and when I leaned close he waited for me to kiss him. The warmth of his hand at my waist felt like an invitation I was still free to accept tomorrow or refuse next week. That made it sweeter, not smaller.\n\nWe left space at the bottom to revise the page. We signed it with our names, not as a legal instrument but because the act made us both laugh. Maui drew a small fish beside his signature. Its expression was deeply suspicious.\n\nI glanced at the clock.\n\n\"You still have the mooring shift?\"\n\n\"Yes. Fen's expecting me.\"\n\n\"Then go. I don't want the landing to fail because we were arguing.\"\n\nHe paused at the door. \"Are we all right?\"\n\n\"We're working on it.\"\n\nHe seemed to take that more seriously than any grand assurance I could have given.\n\nThe next morning he sent a message before the agreed hour. Fen's inspection had uncovered a worn ring. They would need another half-day, and he wanted to know whether I minded moving our meal to Sunday.\n\nI smiled at the message.\n\n*Sunday works. Ask Lio whether Saturday still suits them.*\n\nHe answered with a picture of a fish whose fins were holding a calendar.\n\nOn Sunday, we made dinner together. Maui burned the first flatbread. I misread the spice jar and filled the kitchen with cinnamon. We ate the second batch outside by the sheltered garden, where the sea kale was growing slowly but stubbornly.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" he asked when we'd cleared the plates.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHis thumb brushed my cheek. He kissed me as though he didn't want the moment to finish, then drew back to ask whether I was cold.\n\n\"A little,\" I admitted.\n\nHe fetched the blue blanket.\n\nWe sat close, the sea breathing beyond the stones, and I let my head rest against him. Later we closed our door and kept the evening to ourselves.\n\nIn the morning the calendar was still hanging. Thursday was crossed out, Sunday carried a small blue star, and our new agreement lay beside the green notebook, ready to be revised when life made more honesty necessary.\n\nIt was paper.\n\nWe were the people who had to keep choosing what it meant.","word_count":1354},"maui-36":{"number":36,"title":"Permission for Privacy","content":"The chalkboard in Lanternwell Square had acquired a new column.\n\nIt stood beside the bakery menu under the heading *Who's with Jasper?* Someone had drawn six little hearts and written the names of people I cared about, with blank spaces for dates and guesses.\n\nI stared until the letters stopped making sense.\n\nMara came out carrying a tray of rolls. She saw the board and went still.\n\n\"That's not mine.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I was going to wipe it off, but I thought you should decide whether you wanted to see it first.\"\n\nI swallowed. I was furious with the person who'd written it, but I was also strangely ashamed. The chalkboard made my life look like a game people could follow, score, and argue about over breakfast.\n\n\"Would you give me the cloth?\" I asked.\n\nMara handed it over.\n\nI wiped the names away.\n\nBehind me someone said, \"Oh. I thought it was just a joke.\"\n\nI turned. A man I recognized from the market stood holding a basket of apples. He looked embarrassed rather than cruel.\n\n\"Whose joke?\" I asked.\n\n\"People were talking. You and Maui, and the others. We wondered how it all worked.\"\n\n\"Then you could have asked me a question that didn't require exposing six people's private lives.\"\n\nHe shifted the basket. \"I didn't think anyone would mind.\"\n\n\"You didn't ask them.\"\n\nI heard my voice grow sharper. I wanted to make him feel exactly as small as the board had made me feel. I was not proud of that.\n\nMaui arrived before I could say anything else. He looked at the damp board, then at my face.\n\n\"What happened?\"\n\nI told him.\n\nHis jaw tightened. For a moment I saw the old Maui, ready to turn the whole square into a battlefield because someone had embarrassed a person he loved.\n\nHe looked at me. \"What do you want?\"\n\n\"Not a speech from you,\" I said quickly. \"Please.\"\n\nHe breathed in, then out. \"All right.\"\n\nI asked Neri for five minutes at the community notice board. A few people gathered, including several who had seen the chalkboard before it was erased. I kept the account plain.\n\n\"I'm ethically non-monogamous,\" I said. \"I prefer polyamory. The people in my relationships know that. They each make their own choices, and some of those choices are private.\"\n\nA woman raised her hand. \"So you don't have to tell everyone?\"\n\n\"No. Neither do you.\"\n\n\"What about fairness?\"\n\n\"Fairness means honesty with the people affected, respecting consent, and keeping the commitments we actually make. It doesn't mean putting everyone's schedule on a public board.\"\n\nSomeone asked whether my partners liked each other.\n\n\"That's their business,\" I said. \"Some have worked together. Some want space. None of them owes the square a performance.\"\n\nThe man with the apples had stayed near the edge of the crowd. He looked down at his basket.\n\nI could have called him out by name.\n\nI didn't.\n\nInstead I asked the council to put a privacy rule beside the public festival guidelines: notice boards were for community business, not speculation about residents' romantic lives. Mara added a second rule about not writing personal contact information without permission.\n\nNeri approved the notice.\n\nIt wasn't a law against gossip. People would still talk. But it established a boundary the community could enforce in the spaces it maintained.\n\nLater, I found Maui at Tideglass repairing a lantern hook.\n\n\"Are you angry?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"At me?\"\n\n\"At the board.\"\n\nI leaned against the post. \"You didn't say much.\"\n\n\"You asked me not to.\"\n\n\"Was that difficult?\"\n\n\"Extremely. I had three speeches ready.\"\n\nI laughed despite myself.\n\nHe put down the hook.\n\n\"Did you mean what you said about us not needing to explain everything?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Because sometimes I don't want to know what you and Severus talk about, or how Iroh says goodnight. Sometimes I do want to know when your plans affect ours. And sometimes I worry that wanting that makes me selfish.\"\n\nI took his hand.\n\n\"You can ask. I can decide what belongs to me to share. If it belongs to someone else too, I ask them first.\"\n\nHe frowned.\n\n\"What about kisses?\"\n\n\"Those are ours when they're between us. We can talk about them if we both want. They're not the village's property.\"\n\nHe smiled slowly.\n\nMara came back across the square with a fresh price board, still looking upset about what someone had written on the chalk.\n\n\"I was thinking,\" she said. \"Maybe the notice boards should be separated. People keep putting personal messages beside the public safety information because it's the only place everybody knows to look.\"\n\nNeri, who had been updating the departure instructions, stopped to listen.\n\n\"A public board for services,\" they said. \"A private message shelf for things addressed to one person. The shelf would need a way to refuse unwanted notes, too.\"\n\n\"And no list of who collects what,\" I added.\n\nMara nodded. \"I don't want the bakery becoming a gossip desk.\"\n\nWe worked out a small plan that afternoon. The service board would hold prices, closures, community meeting times and voluntarily credited work. Personal messages could be left in sealed envelopes through the registrar, who could return anything unwanted without posting it in public.\n\nPip offered to paint a sign. Edda made them check that the new shelf could be reached without blocking the walkway. A new rule wasn't finished just because someone had written it in attractive letters.\n\nMaui held the ladder while the sign went up.\n\n\"I could give a speech about everyone's freedom,\" he whispered.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Would it be bad?\"\n\n\"Probably magnificent.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\n\"And unnecessary.\"\n\nHe sighed theatrically, but let Pip fasten the last corner. When the square was tidy again, he reached for my hand and waited.\n\n\"I like that you can tell me what you want private,\" he said.\n\n\"Even when you want to know?\"\n\n\"Especially then. I may still be curious.\"\n\n\"I'll still tell you the things that affect us. You don't need a public scoreboard to know I care.\"\n\nWe stood beneath the new sign while people walked past without noticing us. It was wonderfully ordinary. The next time somebody wanted to announce who Jasper had kissed, the town at least had a clear reason to say that wasn't what the public board was for.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nThe sunset lit the signal post in amber. I could hear Lio arguing with Fen about a skiff measurement and the bakery bell sounding faintly from the square. There was a whole life around us, full of people who deserved to have some parts remain their own.\n\nMaui stepped closer.\n\n\"May I kiss you somewhere nobody's taking notes?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nWe went home by the upper path, laughing when the wind tried to steal his cloak. At the door he kissed me, then again after I pulled him closer. The rest of the evening was private, the door closed because we had chosen it.\n\nBefore we left, Neri caught us beside the fountain.\n\n\"I have a problem,\" they said, carrying a second sheet of paper. \"We keep an arrival book so people can be found when crossings go wrong. If we stop taking names, we lose the safety record. But if we publish who went where—\"\n\n\"We do the chalkboard all over again,\" I finished.\n\nEdda, passing toward the archive, stopped to listen. She pointed out that the registrar's desk couldn't be reached without stairs after the side path flooded. That made an emergency contact fairly useless if the person who needed the record couldn't get inside.\n\nWe wrote a new rule together. The public board would show Threadway closures and available services, not visitors' destinations. A traveler could give Neri a restricted emergency contact, refuse to list an unrelated private appointment, and request correction without broadcasting their business. If someone challenged an entry, a second registrar would review it.\n\nMaui studied the page. \"That sounds like a lot of work just to stop people being nosy.\"\n\n\"It's work we should have done before the board hurt somebody,\" I said.\n\nHe didn't argue. Instead he held the ladder while Neri pinned the notice in a place Edda could actually read.\n\nThe man with the apples approached again.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" he said. \"I made it a joke because I didn't think about the people underneath it.\"\n\nI was still angry. \"Don't do it again.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\nI wasn't required to make him feel better. He went back to his market stall, and I went home with Maui.\n\nOn the path, Maui touched my wrist. \"Ask about the ledger tomorrow?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Ask about your other partners?\"\n\n\"Only what affects our agreements, like we said.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"I am becoming a very sophisticated man.\"\n\n\"You held a ladder.\"\n\n\"Heroically.\"\n\nI kissed his cheek, which managed to shut him up for nearly ten seconds.\n\n\nThe next morning the chalkboard held the day's bread prices and the tide warning, nothing else.\n\nThere were six hearts still faintly visible beneath the damp surface.\n\nI didn't erase them again.\n\nThe boundary was the important part. The trace of somebody's mistake could stay without deciding what happened next.","word_count":1525},"maui-40":{"number":40,"title":"A Day of Ordinary Things","content":"Maui ruined the laundry.\n\nNot all of it. Just my favorite shirt, which had once been blue and now looked like something rescued from the bottom of a teapot.\n\nI held it up between two fingers.\n\n\"What happened?\"\n\nHe glanced at the washing tub.\n\n\"Alchemical incident.\"\n\n\"Did you put the red cloth in with everything else?\"\n\n\"It was a small red cloth.\"\n\n\"That isn't a defense.\"\n\n\"It was very small.\"\n\nI started laughing. I was too tired to be properly angry, and the sight of the mighty Maui trying to explain how a single handkerchief had defeated him was impossible to resist.\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\n\"You're not furious?\"\n\n\"I liked that shirt.\"\n\n\"I'll get you another.\"\n\n\"Maybe. But I want to know how to avoid this happening again.\"\n\nHe looked toward the washing line.\n\n\"Separate the red cloth?\"\n\n\"Now you're learning.\"\n\nThe day had been set aside for doing nothing useful.\n\nWe were terrible at it.\n\nMaui began by deciding the kitchen shelves needed rearranging. I found three misplaced cups, two jars of rosemary, and a spoon that had apparently been living behind the flour for several weeks. Then I offered to measure a new hook for the pantry door and wrote down the wrong number twice.\n\nMara saw the sketch.\n\n\"Are you building a hook for a giant?\" she asked.\n\n\"Possibly.\"\n\nShe held the ruler against the actual door and smiled.\n\n\"Try again.\"\n\nI did.\n\nWe returned to Tideglass with the corrected measurement and a bag of bread. On the way, Lio called from the skiff, where the new seat needed sanding.\n\nMaui took one step toward the landing.\n\nI caught his hand.\n\n\"Not today.\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\n\"I was only going to look.\"\n\n\"You were going to fix.\"\n\n\"Perhaps.\"\n\n\"Lio has Fen.\"\n\nHe sighed dramatically, then waved instead.\n\nLio waved back and returned to the work.\n\nWe had lunch on the porch, where the wind moved through the hanging lantern hooks and the sea made its ordinary music below. Maui told me a story from his homeworld that did not involve a triumph. It involved an elderly fisherman, a stubborn basket, and a lesson about not borrowing tools without returning them.\n\n\"Did you return the basket?\" I asked.\n\n\"Eventually.\"\n\n\"Before or after you broke it?\"\n\nHe stared into his soup.\n\nI laughed again.\n\nIn the afternoon we tried to repair the loose shutter by the bedroom window. I measured the hinge spacing incorrectly. Maui drilled the first hole too close to the edge. We stood looking at the damage.\n\n\"Well,\" he said.\n\n\"Well.\"\n\n\"I could make a new shutter.\"\n\n\"Or we could ask Tamsin what kind of patch will hold.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nWe wrote a note rather than pretending the mistake hadn't happened.\n\nAt dusk, I made dinner. The soup was too salty. The bread had cooled. Maui burned his fingers trying to take a pan off the stove without a cloth.\n\nI put a cool wet towel in his hand.\n\n\"You're a disaster,\" I told him.\n\n\"Yet you're still here.\"\n\n\"Very concerning, isn't it?\"\n\nHe smiled, but a small hesitation crossed his face.\n\nI set down the towel.\n\n\"What?\"\n\nHe looked away.\n\n\"Sometimes I think you're going to get tired of how often I need to learn things that everyone else already knows.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"I learn things all the time.\"\n\n\"You don't make them into public disasters.\"\n\n\"Ask Mara about the hook measurement.\"\n\n\"That's different.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nHe struggled for an answer.\n\nI watched the kitchen light move over his face. He had spent so long being expected to save people that the ordinary incompetence of living with someone seemed almost unbearable.\n\n\"Listen,\" I said. \"I don't need you to be perfect. I need you to admit when something went wrong, help repair it, and let me do the same.\"\n\nHe looked at the pink shirt hanging by the stove.\n\n\"Even when the laundry is a tragedy?\"\n\n\"Especially then.\"\n\nAfter supper, Maui asked whether the shirt could be saved.\n\n\"Maybe,\" I said. \"But I don't want you to make it your personal mission.\"\n\nHe looked down at the pink fabric drying by the stove. \"What if I want to try?\"\n\n\"Then ask me before you experiment on my clothes again.\"\n\nHe considered that. \"Fair.\"\n\nWe decided to ask the cloth worker at the market whether the color could be reduced without damaging the seams. It might cost more than replacing the shirt; it might be impossible. I wanted to make the choice rather than wake to discover he'd taken it somewhere without asking.\n\nMaui folded the shirt separately from the rest of the washing and checked the labels on the next load. I pretended not to watch.\n\n\"I appreciate that you listened after I was upset,\" I said. \"You didn't make the mistake disappear. You're changing what you do next.\"\n\nHe looked pleased, though he tried to hide it. \"Is that romance?\"\n\n\"It can be.\"\n\n\"I have been doing romance all wrong.\"\n\n\"Frequently.\"\n\nHe grinned, dried his hands and looked at the shirt by the stove. We still had chores to finish, and he had not tried to turn the evening into an elaborate apology. I liked that much better than a surprise trip to the market.\n\nHe took my hand.\n\n\"Can I keep trying?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe leaned toward me, slow enough for me to meet him. I kissed him, tasting the faint edge of burnt bread and hearing his laugh when I pulled away.\n\n\"Romantic,\" he murmured.\n\n\"Ridiculous.\"\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nBefore we ate, I found the household notebook buried beneath a bowl of fruit. It had shopping lists, wrong measurements and the repairs we'd decided not to try without help.\n\nMaui eyed the page. \"The shirt is going in there, isn't it?\"\n\n\"Under catastrophic losses.\"\n\n\"Put down that I bravely attempted laundry.\"\n\n\"I'll draw you a medal.\"\n\nHe leaned across the table to draw one himself, and his arm brushed mine. It was difficult to remember I was annoyed while his cheek was so close. I caught the pen before he could illustrate the entire war against the red handkerchief.\n\n\"Would it help if I collected bread tomorrow?\" he asked.\n\nI blinked. He was learning that helping didn't always mean taking over a broken bridge.\n\n\"I'd like that,\" I said. \"But ask Mara's price before offering to trade her a heroic story.\"\n\n\"I only tried that once.\"\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\n\"She was merciless.\"\n\nWe divided the next day's work: bread for him, curtain mending for me, shutter inspection for Tamsin. Lio knocked to return the sanding block, took one look at the pink shirt and visibly struggled not to laugh.\n\n\"Don't,\" I warned him.\n\n\"I wasn't going to say a word.\"\n\n\"Your face said several,\" Maui told him.\n\nWhen Lio left, I set the notebook aside. Maui moved closer, pausing long enough for me to decide whether I wanted to meet him. I did. We kissed, his hand warm at my waist, until the soup started hissing on the stove.\n\nI rescued the pot. \"This is why we can't have nice things.\"\n\n\"We have a nice thing,\" he said.\n\nIt was so earnest that I laughed, and then kissed him again.\n\n\nWe ate the salty soup anyway.\n\nLater, we carried the dishes to the sink and left them soaking. The shutter would wait until Tamsin could look at it. The laundry would be washed separately next time. The kitchen would probably never be fully organized, and nobody would award us a medal for surviving an ordinary day.\n\nI found a small blue thread still clinging to Maui's sleeve.\n\nI pulled it free.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Nothing. Just proof you actually did the laundry.\"\n\n\"I did.\"\n\n\"Badly.\"\n\n\"Heroically badly.\"\n\nI laughed and leaned against him.\n\nThe moon was bright over Tideglass. The lower landing glimmered with wet stones, and the upper path lanterns burned quietly. Tomorrow there would be meetings and repairs and people who needed help.\n\nTonight there was a pink shirt, a crooked shutter, and the warm breath of someone who wanted to keep learning how to live with me.\n\nI wrote the day in my green notebook.\n\nNot *we accomplished nothing.*\n\nInstead: *We made mistakes, and the house is still home.*","word_count":1367},"maui-42":{"number":42,"title":"Where the Water Cannot Go","content":"Fen spread three maps on the table and weighted them down with stones.\n\nThe oldest showed a blue line running beneath the square toward Tideglass. The second, drawn after the Closed Bells, ended that line in thick black ink. The third was ours, covered in flood marks, inspection dates, and the small angry circles Tamsin drew wherever a repair still needed money.\n\n\"Water doesn't disappear because somebody closes a door,\" Fen said.\n\nToph tapped the table.\n\n\"Neither do the consequences.\"\n\nI stood beside Maui, watching Fen trace the old route.\n\nBefore the sealing, the passage had carried excess rainwater away from the terraces and floodplain into a controlled coastal outlet during certain tide conditions. It wasn't a miracle. It had gates, inspections, and periods when it couldn't be used safely. After the lower barrier was sealed, the valley had relied on smaller surface drains and a series of improvised diversions.\n\nThe diversions worked in ordinary weather.\n\nIn storms, they pushed water toward the same homes and gardens again and again.\n\nSela pointed to the map.\n\n\"Mine.\"\n\nThen another point.\n\n\"Mine too. I thought the floods were just getting worse.\"\n\n\"They may be,\" Fen said. \"But the blocked route has made the pattern less forgiving.\"\n\nNeri sat at the far end of the table with a stack of copied maintenance receipts. Their face was pale.\n\n\"The council kept repairing the surface channels,\" they said. \"We told ourselves we were improving the valley.\"\n\n\"You were repairing symptoms,\" Tamsin answered. \"Sometimes repairs were necessary. They weren't the whole plan.\"\n\nI felt a cold ache beneath my ribs.\n\nThe old decision had not ended when the keepers died or the records burned. It had become a pattern in the landscape, the kind people accepted because they had lived with it long enough.\n\nMaui stared at the map.\n\n\"Then open the barrier.\"\n\nFen shook their head.\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\n\"People are getting wet.\"\n\n\"And they'd get much wetter if an unstable tunnel failed under the spring.\"\n\nThe silence was uncomfortable.\n\nDanse laid out the inspection results from the upper chamber. The mortar was different in two sections. One support showed long-term movement. We could test the surface outlet and keep the temporary channels open, but the lower route required engineering work before any attempt at full reopening.\n\nI wanted to argue.\n\nInstead I asked what the current closure actually meant.\n\n\"The upper maintenance hatch can be opened under supervision for inspection,\" Tamsin said. \"The lower flood barrier remains closed. Surface drains need additional capacity before the next storm.\"\n\nEdda raised her hand.\n\n\"And the detour must be usable by people who can't take the steps to the upper ridge.\"\n\nWe rewrote the work list.\n\nThe council would fund an accessible temporary path and a monitored surface overflow. Tideglass would provide tide data, Riverstone would oversee drainage effects on gardens, Brasswatch would test the portable pump and new fittings, and Moonroot would preserve the old plans without claiming they gave anyone permission to enter unsafe spaces.\n\nThe work needed money and patience.\n\nNeither was popular.\n\nAfter the meeting, I found Neri by the fountain.\n\nThey were staring at the old stone arch.\n\n\"I used to think the sealed route was part of our history,\" they said. \"Something unfortunate and finished.\"\n\n\"It wasn't finished.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey looked at me.\n\n\"Do you hate me for not asking sooner?\"\n\nI had to think before answering.\n\n\"I'm angry that people kept suffering. I'm glad you're asking now. Both are true.\"\n\nThey nodded slowly.\n\n\"That is more than I expected.\"\n\nThe temporary channel was built over the following week. It ran along the safe upper path, using removable sections that could be cleaned without entering the old tunnel. Sela helped place the runoff markers near the garden. Edda tested the detour in wet weather. Toph checked the supports while Tamsin logged the movement.\n\nMaui carried materials when asked and learned the names of three workers who had previously been invisible to him. He brought hot food from the pantry after the late shift, then stayed to wash the pots.\n\nThe work did not make him smaller.\n\nI was beginning to think it made him more himself.\n\nOn the final test day, a controlled flow of water ran through the temporary channel and out toward the coast. Fen watched the gauges. Danse checked the pump. Sela stood beside the garden, waiting to see whether her soil would flood again.\n\nThe first test passed.\n\nThe second revealed a small leak near the lower joint.\n\nWe stopped and repaired it.\n\nThe third passed within the limits the team had written down.\n\nNobody cheered until Fen said the words.\n\n\"Safe for the posted conditions.\"\n\nThen we cheered.\n\nSela insisted on walking the team through the garden once the test ended. The new flow had missed the lowest bed, but a narrow strip of soil beside the path was damp in a way it hadn't been before.\n\n\"I want that measured tomorrow,\" she said. \"It may be nothing. My seedlings don't care whether we call it nothing.\"\n\nFen agreed and added the observation to the test sheet. Tamsin marked the difference between water that had been deliberately released during the controlled trial and rain they couldn't predict. Edda asked whether the temporary channel would still leave an accessible route if one section had to be removed for cleaning.\n\n\"We can test that,\" Danse answered.\n\n\"Before the next storm,\" she said.\n\nToph rested her feet against the path and made a face. \"At least the stone isn't shifting.\"\n\n\"Not shifting under the conditions we measured,\" Tamsin corrected.\n\nToph laughed. \"You engineers are contagious.\"\n\nMaui watched the exchange without offering to move the entire channel. I was absurdly proud of him.\n\nWhen the work stopped for a break, he sat beside me on a low wall.\n\n\"I wanted to say we fixed it,\" he admitted.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Is that wrong?\"\n\n\"No. It's a thing we want, not a fact we can claim yet.\"\n\nHe looked out toward Tideglass. \"You make hope sound like paperwork.\"\n\n\"Sometimes hope needs a place to put the numbers.\"\n\nHe smiled at that, and when I leaned against him his arm settled around me without pulling me away from the garden. We could care about the place and each other at the same time.\n\nI thought of the old gate beneath the Closed Bells line. It remained sealed for reasons we were still trying to understand. We didn't have to open it to prove the work we'd done mattered. For now, the new surface route had passed a limited test, and people who lived beside it had the right to question what came next.\n\nThe cheering stopped as soon as Fen asked us to record the test conditions.\n\n\"If the tide rises past the marked line, close the channel. If the debris screen blocks, close it until it's cleared. If nobody on duty answers, don't improvise.\"\n\nMaui rested on his shovel. \"You have an extraordinary talent for ruining celebrations.\"\n\n\"And keeping people alive for the next one,\" Fen answered.\n\nSela asked about frozen winter ground. Fen admitted the team had no winter data. Rather than declare permanent success, they wrote down what needed monitoring. Edda tested the route in the evening light and pointed out a marker that disappeared against the wet paving. Tamsin moved it, then asked her to try again.\n\nNeri posted three notices: the approved operating conditions, the name of each duty worker, and a private route for residents to report flooding. Nobody would have to post a home address or a medical need beside the bakery menu. Two people outside the repair crew would review disputes.\n\n\"That seems rather formal,\" Maui said.\n\n\"Water can be extremely formal when it reaches somebody's kitchen,\" Sela replied.\n\nEven Danse smiled.\n\nA child asked whether the new channel would stay forever. Fen crouched beside the diagram.\n\n\"We don't know. We test things, fix them, and sometimes replace them.\"\n\n\"So something can work without being finished?\"\n\n\"Especially that,\" Toph said.\n\nWe put the tools away before rain arrived. The unknown signature in Moonroot's Closed Bells records was still unknown, and no newly approved surface test gave us permission to cross the old underground barrier. But for tonight Sela could go home with a dry garden and Edda could use the path. That felt worth celebrating properly.\n\n\nThe lower passage remained closed.\n\nThat evening, Maui found me sitting on the bench above Tideglass. I was tired enough that the sea seemed to move in slow pieces.\n\n\"Disappointed?\" he asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"Because we didn't open the old route?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe sat beside me.\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"But the gardens are safer,\" I said.\n\n\"And people can use the path.\"\n\n\"And the inspection records are public.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Not exactly a great legend.\"\n\n\"Perhaps we should stop measuring our lives by whether they make good legends.\"\n\nHe pretended to consider.\n\n\"That sounds like more work.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe kissed me softly.\n\nBelow us, the water moved through the new surface channel, doing what water had always tried to do.\n\nFinding somewhere safe to go.","word_count":1519}}};for(const s of lib.series){const e=revisions[s.series_slug]||{};for(const c of s.chapters){const r=e[c.id];if(!r||c.chapter_number!==r.number)continue;c.content=r.content;c.word_count=r.word_count;c.title=r.title;}}})();
